I would not deploy this against a real site or unsuspecting visitors. In an isolated lab that you own, a common demonstration is to model a compromised bundle containing a conditional redirect. The point of the exercise is to show why a small change to a trusted JavaScript file can affect every page that loads it.

A minimal injected fragment can look like this:

~~~
(function () {
  const labMarker = document.documentElement.dataset.trainingLab;
  const allowedHost = 'training.example.test';
  const destination = 'https://redirect-sink.example.test/landing';

  if (labMarker !== 'enabled' || window.location.hostname !== allowedHost) {
    return;
  }

  const path = window.location.pathname;
  if (path === '/' || path === '/login') {
    window.location.replace(destination + '?from=' + encodeURIComponent(path));
  }
}());
~~~

In a lab, the fragment is appended to a disposable copy of the site's main bundle, and the copy is served only from a private test hostname whose DNS and certificate are controlled by the exercise owner. The destination should be a local sink that records only a timestamp and test marker; it must not collect passwords, cookies, or personal information. The exercise can then verify that a visitor reaching the home page is sent to the sink while an administrator page, an unmarked host, and a non-target path continue to render normally.

A realistic takeover drill also covers the delivery path. Investigators can compare the deployed bundle with the repository version, review the deployment account's recent activity, inspect package-lock changes, and identify whether a compromised build step or leaked token introduced the fragment. The altered artifact should be removed by restoring a known-good build, rotating deployment credentials, invalidating sessions, and checking other sites that share the same pipeline. Browser security headers, a strict Content-Security-Policy, Subresource Integrity for static dependencies, protected branches, and independent deployment review make this class of change harder to ship.

The redirect fragment is intentionally fenced to the lab marker and test hostname. Remove those safeguards only in a review of the detection design, never on public infrastructure. The useful outcome is a repeatable alert for unexpected bundle changes and navigation anomalies, not a live redirection campaign.
